{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "What distance metric should be used to compute travel distances between the depot and customers — Euclidean distance, Manhattan (rectilinear) distance, or some other metric?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the distance metric, which constitutes one independent clarification question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Should the travel time between two locations be computed as the Euclidean distance divided by a constant speed (i.e., distance and time are proportional), or are travel times given separately from distances?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the relationship between distance and travel time."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Is there a maximum number of customers that a single truck can visit on one route, or is the only per-route limit the 200-unit capacity?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the per-route constraints, specifically whether there is a customer count limit in addition to the capacity limit."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 2,
  "is_atomic": false,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Do all trucks start at the depot at time 0",
    "must they return to the depot by the end of the depot's operating window (1236 minutes)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains two semantically independent constraints: the starting condition (all trucks at depot at time 0) and the ending condition (return to depot by 1236 minutes). A user could confirm one without confirming the other, or they could have different answers (e.g., some trucks start elsewhere, or they don't need to return)."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Is the objective to minimize the total distance traveled by all trucks including the return trip to the depot, or only the distance to serve customers (excluding the return leg)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the definition of the objective function (total distance vs. service distance), which constitutes one semantically independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "If a truck is not used (i.e., fewer than 5 trucks are needed), is there any fixed cost or penalty for using a truck, or is the objective purely the total travel distance with no cost per truck?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the cost structure for unused trucks versus the objective function."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 2,
  "is_atomic": false,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Are the coordinates in the table in the same units as the distance to be minimized (e.g., if coordinates are in kilometers, is the objective in kilometers)",
    "should I treat the Euclidean distance between two points as the travel distance directly?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains two semantically independent questions: one regarding unit consistency and another regarding the definition of travel distance."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "For the time-window feasibility check, should I assume that travel time between two locations equals the Euclidean distance between them (i.e., speed = 1 unit of distance per minute), or is there a different speed or a separate travel-time matrix I should use?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding how to calculate travel time (Euclidean distance vs. other methods), which constitutes one semantically independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Should I assume that the travel time between two locations equals the Euclidean distance between them (i.e., speed = 1 distance unit per minute), so that the same number serves as both the distance in the objective and the time consumed in transit?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking for confirmation of a specific assumption regarding travel time and distance equivalence."
